The microphone is actually opened
=================================

Dictation read a transcript that somebody else was producing. The talk service does not open the
microphone — it transcribes what it is fed, and it keeps one owner at a time — so the button said
Listening while nothing was ever captured, and the transcript stayed empty until some other tool
happened to be recording. That reads exactly like "speech is broken", and it was not the recogniser.

The engine now owns the microphone for as long as the switch is on: claim the recognizer, run
`parecord --raw --format=s16le --rate 16000 --channels=1 --latency-msec=100`, post each 100 ms block
to `/chunk`, and release on stop. Two details that cost a round trip each:

* the talk service reads the owner name from the **JSON body** of `/claim` and from the **query** of
  `/chunk`. Sending it in the wrong place claims the microphone as `anon` and then has every chunk
  refused as somebody else's — a capture loop that runs and delivers nothing;
* claim before the microphone opens. A chunk that arrives first is refused, and the first chunk of a
  sentence is the one with the first word in it.

`Voice.startDictation`, `Voice.stopDictation` and `Voice.dictating` are the new IPC calls; the Mic
switch in the composer drives them, and reports the engine's own error if the microphone will not
open instead of flipping to Listening and lying.

